### ============================================================
### Auth Rosetta - API tests (cookie-based session)
###
### The token now lives in an HttpOnly cookie named "access_token".
### Your HTTP client stores the cookie from the login response and sends
### it on later requests automatically (VS Code REST Client does this by
### default). Because of that, ORDER MATTERS: anonymous checks must run
### BEFORE the successful login, and the revocation replay needs the
### token value pasted in by hand.
###
### How to use:
###   1. Start the backend and set @baseUrl for the stack under test.
###   2. Run the requests top to bottom.
###   3. At step 4, paste the secret into an authenticator app, then put the
###      current 6-digit code into @code. Codes last 30 seconds, so update
###      @code right before steps 4 and 6.
###   4. To re-run the whole flow, change @email (register returns 409
###      otherwise) and clear your client's cookies.
### ============================================================

# Backend ports: .NET 5001 | NestJS 5002 | Python 5003 | Go 5004 | Java 5005
@baseUrl = http://localhost:5001
@email = test@example.com
@password = Sup3rSecret!
@code = 548339

# Paste the cookie VALUE from the step 6 response here (see step 6).
@stolenToken = paste-token-value-here


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 0. ANONYMOUS CHECKS (run these before logging in)
### ------------------------------------------------------------

### 0a. /me without a cookie -> expect 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/me

### 0b. /me with a garbage cookie -> expect 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/me
Cookie: access_token=not-a-real-token

### 0c. /logout without a cookie -> expect 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout

### 0d. Register with a short password -> expect 400
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "short"
}

### 0e. Register with an empty email -> expect 400
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 1. REGISTER -> expect 201 with { otpauthUri, secret }
### Copy the "secret" into your authenticator app (manual entry),
### or paste the otpauthUri into a QR generator.
### ------------------------------------------------------------

# @name register
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 1b. Register the same email again -> expect 409
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 2. LOGIN BEFORE 2FA IS CONFIRMED -> expect 401, no Set-Cookie
### ------------------------------------------------------------

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "code": "{{code}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 3. CONFIRM 2FA WITH A WRONG CODE -> expect 400
### ------------------------------------------------------------

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/2fa/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "code": "111111"
}

### 3b. Confirm 2FA for an unknown email -> expect 400 (same as a wrong code)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/2fa/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody@example.com",
  "code": "{{code}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 4. CONFIRM 2FA WITH A VALID CODE -> expect 200
### Update @code at the top first.
### ------------------------------------------------------------

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/2fa/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "code": "{{code}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 5. LOGIN FAILURES - all three must return an identical 401
###    and must NOT set a cookie
### ------------------------------------------------------------

### 5a. Wrong password
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "WrongPassword123",
  "code": "{{code}}"
}

### 5b. Wrong code
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "code": "111111"
}

### 5c. Unknown email
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody@example.com",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "code": "{{code}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 6. SUCCESSFUL LOGIN -> expect 200 with { message }
### Update @code at the top if the previous one is older than 30 seconds.
###
### Check the RESPONSE HEADERS:
###   - Set-Cookie: access_token=...; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict; Path=/
###   - the body must NOT contain a token
###
### Then copy the cookie value (the part after "access_token=" and
### before the first ";") into @stolenToken at the top. Step 9 uses it
### to prove the token is revoked server-side.
### ------------------------------------------------------------

# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "code": "{{code}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 7. ME with the session cookie -> expect 200 with { email }
### The cookie is sent automatically, there is no Authorization header.
### ------------------------------------------------------------

GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/me


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 8. LOGOUT -> expect 204
### The response must contain a Set-Cookie that clears access_token.
### ------------------------------------------------------------

POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout

### 8b. /me again -> expect 401 (the cookie was cleared)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/me


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 9. REVOCATION REPLAY
### Simulates a stolen token: send the OLD cookie value manually.
### Both must return 401, because its jti is on the denylist.
### ------------------------------------------------------------

### 9a. Replay the revoked token on /me -> expect 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/me
Cookie: access_token={{stolenToken}}

### 9b. Replay the revoked token on /logout -> expect 401
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout
Cookie: access_token={{stolenToken}}
